living in the city can be stressful I  often find myself longing to get out  into the countryside in fact there's  plenty of evidence to suggest that this  is good for your health above and beyond  the effects of the exercise but what if  you can't get out to experience these  environments can they be brought to you  professor Bob stone is working on doing  just that and I'm going to his lab to  give it a go well we're really concerned  about the number of people who can't go  out for walks people in hospices care  homes veterans coming back from  Afghanistan and who find themselves with  amputated limbs and so on so with  virtual environments we're able to use  head mounted displays or large screens  and actually expose them to virtual  reconstructions of well-known beauty  spots not necessary in this country but  further abroad as well and how do you  actually build the world what we've done  with with these worlds because they're  geographically based is we've actually  bought the digital train data from a  geographical source and aerial  photography so we would combine those  two in the games engine software that  powers this this this this particular  environment and then we use it as a  template to populate it with man-made  objects rivers trees bushes flowers and  so on so the world that we're going to  be showing you today is based on Wembley  Bay which is a lovely piece of coastline  just east of Plymouth it's got both  fantastic coastal environment it's got  some fabulous forestry environments just  slightly inland and it is a perfect  place to go and just literally chill out  which is what I did many moons ago when  I was doing my a-level revision you  right-handed or left-handed you're  right-handed okay what we need to do is  to put these devices onto your left hand  this is the galvanic skin response meter  which if we make sure that goes right  down to the bottom of your at the base  of your fingers that will be measuring  your skin response and so we're looking  at the conductance and measuring the  trend over time and this is a pulse  oximeter which we'll be measuring  amongst other things your heart rate so  we can put it onto your index finger and  we can start that let's make sure the  thing is nice and snug in there and we  can read it of your pulse right let's  see how stressed I really am first thing  we have to do is to raise your arousal  level so that we can do some comparisons  afterwards so how good these event these  environments are we're just looking at  your mental ability to rotate in this  case the two-dimensional objects  what happens if I get one wrong give me  an electric shock no nothing is  ethically awkward is that that wouldn't  be a nice great let's have a look at  this world okay so how am I actually  going to walk around this world well  we're gonna give you this little device  which is commercially available it's  called a Zeki you move forwards and  backwards using the thumb stick you look  left and right using the thumb stick and  then if you want to look up and down  these little buttons on the front maybe  you should look down and - and to look  up so gives you complete freedom to  explore the virtual world the sound is  designed such a way that the effects are  triggered and the volume is triggered  when you approach a particular zone or  object and if I'm one of the  participants in your study what would  you actually have me do in this world  we'd ask you do two things number one is  we'd ask you to walk around for about 60  seconds take in the view and then choose  where you would like to sit or to stand  virtually so that you could actually get  the full benefit of the environment then  we do is expose you to different times  of day so for example we can have a nice  sunrise at dawn we can have a nice  sunset at dusk and we expose you to  various different environments and  various different environmental effects  we try to put in borders so that you can  for example for off the end of the  virtual world which is not very not very  restorative and presumably it's not like  a video game where you want people to be  able to run miles and miles in seconds  I think at the moment it's probably just  a little too fast we want this to be a  restorative gentle walk on the  countryside so we need to match the the  sound of the footsteps that you're  hearing which are actually important for  creating the news that you're immersed  in the world with the speed of movement  across the screen so how does this  environment make you feel it's actually  amazingly relaxing that's often the  response we get we people will sit back  and the sounds in particular they will  sit back and listen and get almost  soaked in immersed in the environment  and we always check up on how well you  really are feeling because my colleague  Chang is monitoring your heart rate and  your skin response your arousal levels  in real-time with the the laptops that  he's got in front of him as he actually  dropped it's gone down to continue okay  it's not gonna be easy taking this into  a hospital setting we have a lot of  things to consider things like ethics  things like hygiene the chances are we  will probably not be using head mounted  displays but using large screens that we  can weave into patients beds but it's  quite a challenge getting this  technology into a sterile setting such  as a hospital well we're getting a lot  of interest from non-military and and  and the medical sectors palliative care  end-of-life care vascular surgeons are  interested we're even seeing interest  for example from accident emergency Wars  could this be used to calm violent  patients who come into the wards on a  Friday or Saturday for example we hope  to get the technology into a hospices  and care homes on large screens possibly  even on portable computers like like  iPads or or Android tablets so the sky's  the limit  professor stone and his team are set to  test the virtual worlds over